<institution>Department of Medicine, Li Ka Shing Faculty of Medicine, The University of Hong Kong, Queen Mary Hospital</institution>, <addr-line>102 Pokfulam Road, Hong Kong SAR</addr-line>, <country country="CN">China</country></aff></contrib></contrib-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="editor"><name><surname>Gimelli</surname><given-names>Alessia</given-names></name><role>Handling Editor</role></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="oead021-cor1">Corresponding author. Tel: +86-755-8691-3333, Fax: +852-2855-1652, Email: <email>myng2@hku.hk</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>3</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub" iso-8601-date="2023-03-07"><day>07</day><month>3</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>07</day><month>3</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>2</issue><elocation-id>oead021</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>28</day><month>9</month><year>2022</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>27</day><month>2</month><year>2023</year></date><date date-type="editorial-decision"><day>01</day><month>3</month><year>2023</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>03</day><month>3</month><year>2023</year></date><date date-type="corrected-typeset"><day>27</day><month>3</month><year>2023</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2023. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the European Society of Cardiology.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2023</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link>), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. For commercial re-use, please contact journals.permissions@oup.com</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="oead021.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><sec id="s1"><title>Aims</title><p>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) continues to be a diagnostic challenge. Cardiac magnetic resonance atrial measurement, feature tracking (CMR-FT), tagging has long been suggested to diagnose HFpEF and potentially complement echocardiography especially when echocardiography is indeterminate. Data supporting the use of CMR atrial measurements, CMR-FT or tagging, are absent. Our aim is to conduct a prospective case-control study assessing the diagnostic accuracy of CMR atrial volume/area, CMR-FT, and tagging to diagnose HFpEF amongst patients suspected of having HFpEF.</p></sec><sec id="s2"><title>Methods and results</title><p>One hundred and twenty-one suspected HFpEF patients were prospectively recruited from four centres. Patients underwent echocardiography, CMR, and N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide (NT-proBNP) measurements within 24&#x02005;h to diagnose HFpEF. Patients without HFpEF diagnosis underwent catheter pressure measurements or stress echocardiography to confirm HFpEF or non-HFpEF. Area under the curve (AUC) was determined by comparing HFpEF with non-HFpEF patients. Fifty-three HFpEF (median age 78 years, interquartile range 74&#x02013;82 years) and thirty-eight non-HFpEF (median age 70 years, interquartile range 64&#x02013;76 years) were recruited. Cardiac magnetic resonance left atrial (LA) reservoir strain (ResS), LA area index (LAAi), and LA volume index (LAVi) had the highest diagnostic accuracy (AUCs 0.803, 0.815, and 0.776, respectively). Left atrial ResS, LAAi, and LAVi had significantly better diagnostic accuracy than CMR-FT left ventricle (LV)/right ventricle (RV) parameters and tagging (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.01). Tagging circumferential and radial strain had poor diagnostic accuracy (AUC 0.644 and 0.541, respectively).</p></sec><sec id="s3"><title>Conclusion</title><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance LA ResS, LAAi, and LAVi have the highest diagnostic accuracy to identify HFpEF patients from non-HFpEF patients amongst clinically suspected HFpEF patients. Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking LV/RV parameters and tagging had low diagnostic accuracy to diagnose HFpEF.</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical"><title>Graphical Abstract</title><p>
<fig position="anchor" id="oead021-oead021_ga1"><label>Graphical Abstract</label><graphic xlink:href="oead021_ga1" position="float"/></fig>
</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction</kwd><kwd>Cardiac magnetic resonance</kwd><kwd>Feature tracking</kwd><kwd>Left atrium</kwd><kwd>Strain</kwd><kwd>Tagging</kwd></kwd-group><counts><page-count count="11"/></counts></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro" id="oead021-s0"><title>Introduction</title><p>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) makes up approximately 50% of heart failure patients<sup><xref rid="oead021-B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup> and is increasing in prevalence.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref></sup> Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction diagnostic criteria are signs/symptoms of heart failure, left ventricular (LV) ejection fraction (LVEF) &#x02265; 50%, and evidence of LV diastolic dysfunction or LV/left atrial (LA) changes indicating diastolic dysfunction on echocardiography or cardiac catheterization.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup></p><p>To treat HFpEF, effective diagnostic tools are required. Currently, echocardiography is the investigation of choice because it is non-invasive and easily available.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup> However, non-diagnostic/indeterminate echocardiograms<sup><xref rid="oead021-B4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup> lead to further tests which include invasive pressure measurements. Ideally, an alternative non-invasive modality should be utilized as the complication risks from right- or left-sided cardiac catheterization (e.g. pneumothorax, cerebrovascular accident, myocardial infarction, and death) although low (1.1&#x02013;1.7%) are not negligible.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref></sup> However, right-sided cardiac catheterization has lower risk than left-sided catheterization.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref></sup></p><p>Cardiovascular magnetic resonance imaging (CMR) is the gold standard for ventricular systolic function assessment (i.e. ejection fraction) and ventricular volumes due to its superior inter and intraobserver variability<sup><xref rid="oead021-B7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref></sup> compared to echocardiography, which is highly operator dependent. In terms of diastolic function assessment, tagging has been proposed.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup> However, this requires extra sequences, requiring more scanning/post-processing time and specialized post-processing software. This has restricted tagging&#x02019;s use in the normal clinical workflow. Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking (CMR-FT) is a relatively new method which involves assessing myocardial strain on the standard cine images which are routinely acquired (<italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-F1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref></italic>) and therefore negates the requirement of extra sequences and longer scanning times. This technique identifies the myocardial &#x02018;features&#x02019; in CMR cine images to determine myocardial deformation and thereby calculate strain or strain rate (SR) of the myocardium globally or segmentally. This allows direct measurements of myocardial relaxation. Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking has been shown to be comparable to CMR tagging but with shorter post-processing time<sup><xref rid="oead021-B10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> and no extra sequences required. Therefore, CMR&#x02013;FT has potential to be used in routine clinical work-flow. Furthermore, CMR&#x02013;FT has been shown to identify and correlate with diastolic dysfunction on echocardiography<sup><xref rid="oead021-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup> but whether this is accurate at identifying HFpEF has not been determined. Lastly, CMR atrial measurements have also been suggested to diagnose HFpEF but its diagnostic accuracy is unknown.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup></p><fig position="float" id="oead021-F1" fig-type="figure"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction and non-heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patient. Images (<italic toggle="yes">A</italic>) and (<italic toggle="yes">B</italic>) show contouring of the left atrium in the two- and four-chamber cines for left atrial strain. Images (<italic toggle="yes">C</italic>) and (<italic toggle="yes">D</italic>) show the radial and longitudinal strain assessment in the short-axis and four-chamber cines using cardiovascular magnetic resonance feature tracking. Images (<italic toggle="yes">E</italic>) and (<italic toggle="yes">F</italic>) show contouring of the left and right atria in the two- and four-chamber cines in end-systole. Note that the heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patient has lower left atrial strain (reservoir, conduit, and booster) and larger left atrial volume/area compared to the non-heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patient.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="oead021f1" position="float"/></fig><p>This study aims to determine if atrial size/volumes, CMR-FT, and tagging can diagnose HFpEF.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods" id="oead021-s1"><title>Methods</title><p>Hong Kong West-Cluster Institutional review board provided research ethics approval. This prospective case-control study is registered on ClinicalTrials.gov(No. NCT04063579). Informed consent was obtained from all subjects.</p><sec id="oead021-s1.1"><title>Patient categories</title><p>Three groups were recruited (HFpEF patients, non-HFpEF patients, and normal volunteers) from September 2018 to December 2021. The HFpEF and non-HFpEF patients were recruited from the wards and outpatient clinics of four hospitals, three of which are community hospitals and the other is a tertiary centre. The normal volunteers were recruited via public advertisements. See <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary methods</xref> for sample size calculation.</p><p>Normal volunteers were asymptomatic subjects with no significant previous cardiac history. Exclusion criteria were contraindication to contrast-enhanced CMR study, estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) &#x0003c; 30&#x02005;mL/min/1.73 m<sup>2</sup>, and more than moderate valvular disease (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref></italic> for CONSORT diagram).</p><fig position="float" id="oead021-F2" fig-type="figure"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>CONSORT diagram. ESC, European Society of Cardiology; HCM, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; HCM, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; HFpEF, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, HF, heart failure; * = one patient undertook both stress echocardiography and invasive catheter pressure measurement. &#x02020; = six patients identified on invasive catheterization and one patient identified on stress echocardiography.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="oead021f2" position="float"/></fig><p>Suspected HFpEF patients were identified from inpatient and outpatient settings. In the majority of cases, a previous echocardiogram had been performed confirming LVEF &#x02265;50% prior to referral by clinical colleagues. Inclusion criteria were symptoms/signs of heart failure. Exclusion criteria were previous cardiac imaging indicating significant coronary artery disease, severe pulmonary disease (i.e. FEV1 &#x0003c; 50% predicted), cardiomyopathy, and constrictive pericarditis.</p><p>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patients were diagnosed using the 2021 European Society of Cardiology (ESC) Heart Failure Guidelines.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> Previous studies have shown that the HFA-PEFF<sup><xref rid="oead021-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> and H2PEF scores<sup><xref rid="oead021-B16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref></sup> have limitations when applied in an Asian setting due to lower obesity/atrial fibrillation and smaller heart sizes in Asian populations<sup><xref rid="oead021-B17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref></sup> and thus were not utilized for this study. Briefly, the ESC criteria are (i) symptoms and signs of heart failure, (ii) &#x02265; 50% LVEF, (iii) cardiac structural and/or functional abnormalities consistent with LV diastolic dysfunction or raised LV filling pressure (see paragraph below for fulfilling criteria for LV diastolic dysfunction or raised LV filling pressure), and (iv) raised N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide (NT-proBNP) level.</p><p>To fulfil the LV diastolic dysfunction or raised LV filling pressure, one of the following echocardiography criteria needed to be fulfilled (a) LV mass index &#x02265;95&#x02005;g/m<sup>2</sup> (female), &#x02265; 115&#x02005;g/m<sup>2</sup> (male), (b) left atrial volume index &#x0003e;34&#x02005;mL/m<sup>2</sup> (sinus rhythm) (in the presence of AF, the threshold is &#x0003e;40&#x02005;mL/m<sup>2</sup>, <italic toggle="yes">E</italic>/<italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; &#x0003e; 9), (c) tricuspid regurgitation velocity at rest &#x0003e;2.8&#x02005;m/s, (d) pulmonary artery systolic pressure &#x0003e;35 mmHg, (e) septal wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; velocity &#x0003c;7&#x02005;cm/s, and (f) lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; velocity &#x0003c;10&#x02005;cm/s.</p><p>Non-HFpEF patients were classified as patients suspected of having HFpEF but did not fulfil ESC 2021 criteria for HFpEF. All non-HFpEF patients were invited for subsequent invasive pressure measurements or stress echocardiography (see <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary methods</xref> for details). If these tests confirmed diastolic dysfunction, these patients were confirmed as HFpEF. These non-HFpEF patients were thought to be largely due to deconditioning.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.2"><title>Stress echocardiography</title><p>Stress echocardiography was performed by an experienced stress echocardiographer using a semi-supine bicycle. Stress was achieved using a step-wise protocol as recommended by the ESC guidelines.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> Diagnostic criteria for stress echocardiography utilized the 2021 ESC HFpEF criteria.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> Diastolic dysfunction was diagnosed when average <italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; ratio at peak stress &#x02265;15 or tricuspid regurgitation velocity at peak stress &#x0003e;3.4&#x02005;m/s.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> Exercise testing started with a warm-up period of 5&#x02005;min at 25 W. Thereafter, the workload was increased by 25 W every 3&#x02005;min until the individual reached his predicted peak heart rate and/or developed limiting symptoms. Apical four-chamber and parasternal short-axis cine-loops (M-mode/B-mode/pulsed Doppler) were recorded via high-resolution ultrasound (Mindray M9, Hong Kong) at baseline, at each stage of incremental exercise, as well as during the first 2&#x02005;min of the recovery phase (when mitral E and A velocities are no longer fused and left ventricular filling pressures remain elevated).<sup><xref rid="oead021-B18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref></sup></p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.3"><title>Invasive pressure measurements</title><p>Left ventricular volume and pressure study was undertaken according to the European Society of Cardiology (ESC) diagnostic criteria, and methods<sup><xref rid="oead021-B19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref></sup> for establishing diastolic dysfunction were utilized. Invasive pressure measurements were performed with a Mikro-Cath pressure catheter (Millar Inc., Texas, USA) to produce LV pressure graphs. The criteria for diastolic dysfunction are the presence of one of the following: (i) Time constant of LV relaxation (&#x003c4;) &#x0003e; 49 ms; (ii) LV end-diastolic pressure &#x0003e;16 mmHg, or (iii) LV chamber stiffness constant (&#x003b2;) &#x0003e; 0.27. Nine patients underwent invasive pressure measurements.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.4"><title>CMR examination</title><p>All subjects recruited underwent contrast CMR and echocardiography within 24&#x02005;h period. Full blood count, eGFR, and NT-proBNP were measured at the time of the CMR examination.</p><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance was performed with a 3&#x02005;T Philips Achieva scanner (Philips, Amsterdam, Netherlands) and a 3&#x02005;T GE Premier scanner (General Electric, Boston, USA). Details on sequence parameters and planning positions can be found in the <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary methods</xref>.</p><p>The following images were acquired: two-chamber, three-chamber, four-chamber, short-axis oblique cine and late gadolinium enhancement images, tagging in the short-axis plane in the LV basal, mid-ventricular, and apical segments.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.5"><title>Cardiac magnetic resonance analysis</title><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance analysis was performed by blinded dedicated CMR analysts and a Level 3 accredited CMR expert with &#x0003e;10 years of experience. cvi42 software (Circle Inc, Calgary, Canada) was used to determine/analyse CMR LV/right ventricular (RV) volumes, LV/RV ejection fraction (EF), atrial measurements, and strain. See <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary methods</xref> for further details.</p><p>Maximal LA volume index (LAVi) to body surface area (BSA) was performed using the biplane area method.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> Left atrial and right atrial (RA) area measurements were measured in the four-chamber cine in the LV end-systolic phase.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking LV longitudinal strain (LS) and SR were performed on the two- and four-chamber cine views. For LV radial and circumferential strain, these were derived from the short-axis cine images (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-F1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref></italic>). For RV LS, the four-chamber cine images were utilized as previously described.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref></sup> Short-axis cine was used for RV radial strain. Early diastolic SR was identified as the first peak during the diastolic phase.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref></sup> Left atrial strain was performed on the two- and four-chamber cine views. Left atrial reservoir (ResS), booster (BoS), and conduit strain (CoS) were defined and analysed as previously described.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref></sup></p><p>Tagging images were analysed on VIRTUE 5.51 software (Myocardial Solutions, Morrisville, North Carolina, USA). Circumferential strain and radial strain were calculated for the basal, mid-ventricular, and apical slices.</p><p>Interobserver variability assessment was performed on 30 randomly chosen cases. Both observers performed contouring separately &#x0003e;4 weeks apart.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.6"><title>Echocardiography examination</title><p>Echocardiography was performed using a GE Vivid E95 or GE Vivid IQ echocardiography machine (General Electric Vingmed. Ultrasound, Milwaukee, WI, USA) with the patient lying in the lateral decubitus position. Examinations were performed by a dedicated echocardiographer using a 3.5&#x02005;MHz transducer. Off-line analysis was performed using EchoPAC version 108.1.5 (General Electric Vingmed, Horten, Norway). LV diastolic indices were acquired using pulsed wave tissue Doppler imaging. Septal and lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; velocities, mitral E-wave and A-wave, tricuspid regurgitation peak velocity (TRPV), LAVi, and LV mass index (LVMi) were acquired. Left atrial volume index was assessed in the apical two- and four-chamber view and indexed to BSA. Left ventricular mass index was calculated according to the Devereux formula and indexed to BSA.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref></sup></p></sec><sec id="oead021-s1.7"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p>Strain and SR values were stated as positive numbers for ease of analysis and interpretation. Variables were assessed for normality using a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. Variables were compared between two groups using Student&#x02019;s <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>-tests or Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test if the variables were normally or not normally distributed. For comparison of variables in multiple groups Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis test was used due to the small numbers in the normal volunteer group. Receiver operating characteristic analysis was performed to determine the area under the curve (AUC) and diagnostic accuracy of CMR parameters to diagnose HFpEF. Delong tests were performed to determine differences in AUC. Multivariable logistic regression was used to assess if the CMR parameters remained significantly different between the HFpEF and non-HFpEF cohort. The models included age, sex, diuretic, body mass index, atrial fibrillation, and estimated glomerular function. Intraclass correlation coefficient was performed to assess interobserver variability. <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>-value &#x0003c;0.05 was regarded as statistically significant.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="oead021-s2"><title>Results</title><p>One hundred and forty-two subjects were initially recruited. After application of exclusion criteria, stress echocardiography, and invasive catheter pressure measurements, there were 53 HFpEF, 17 normal volunteers, and 38 non-HFpEF patients (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-F1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref></italic> CONSORT diagram).</p><sec id="oead021-s2.1"><title>Demographics and cardiac magnetic resonance parameters</title><p>Patient characteristics, cardiac risk factors, medications, co-morbidities, blood markers, and echocardiography parameters for diagnosing HFpEF are listed in <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></italic>. Briefly, there were significant differences between HFpEF and normal volunteers in age, body mass index, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes, beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, diuretics, aspirin, anti-diabetics, statins, atrial fibrillation, NT-pro-BNP, and eGFR of the patients/volunteers across the groups. There were significant differences between HFpEF and non-HFpEF patients in terms of age, beta-blockers, diuretic, statin, atrial fibrillation, NT-proBNP, and eGFR.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="oead021-T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Patient characteristics of three patient groups which are heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, normal volunteers, and non-heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patients</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col valign="top" align="left" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 53)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal volunteers (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 17)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Non-HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 38)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">78 (74&#x02013;82)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64 (60&#x02013;67)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">70 (64&#x02013;76)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Female (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">55.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.785</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">BMI</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">26.1 (23.6&#x02013;29.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.7 (19.6&#x02013;23.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">27.4 (24.0&#x02013;31.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Risk factors</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Hypertension (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">88.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.88</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">73.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Hyperlipidaemia (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">44.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.005<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Smoking (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.10</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Diabetes (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">41.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.006<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Medications</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;ACEI/ARB (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">18.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.044<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Beta blockers (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">52.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Calcium Channel blockers (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">67.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Diuretics (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">39.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Clopidogrel (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.225</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Aspirin (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">32.1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">39.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.011<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Anti-diabetics (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">34.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.023<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Statins (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">47.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Other Co-morbidities</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Previous coronary artery stents (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.287</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Atrial fibrillation (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">39.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Blood results</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Haemoglobin (g/dL)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.6 (11.6&#x02013;13.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (12.6&#x02013;14.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.3 (12.0&#x02013;14.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.194</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;NT-ProBNP (pg/mL)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">550.1 (196.6&#x02013;1016.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">35.2 (31.4&#x02013;55.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">66.0 (30.9&#x02013;91.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Estimated GFR (mL/min/1.73 m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60 (46&#x02013;79)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">92 (87&#x02013;104)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">81 (63&#x02013;89)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Echocardiography parameters</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV mass index (g/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">113.9 (91.4&#x02013;132.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.9 (60.6&#x02013;88.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">98.4 (85.5&#x02013;118.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Left atrial volume index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">51.6 (38.1&#x02013;65.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.3 (24.0&#x02013;37.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">37.2 (31&#x02013;41.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;<italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; ratio at rest</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.3 (9.6&#x02013;14.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.4 (5.0&#x02013;9.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.0 (8.8&#x02013;12.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Tricuspid regurgitation velocity at rest (m/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.76 (2.32&#x02013;3.02)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.52 (0.00&#x02013;2.30)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.18 (0.00&#x02013;2.53)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Pulmonary artery systolic pressure (mmHg)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">35 (26&#x02013;40)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (0&#x02013;25)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24 (0&#x02013;30)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn4" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Septal wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; velocity (m/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.06 (0.05&#x02013;0.08)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.08 (0.07&#x02013;0.10)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.07 (0.06&#x02013;0.08)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.026<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; velocity</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.08 (0.07&#x02013;0.10)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.11 (0.09&#x02013;0.12)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.08 (0.06&#x02013;0.09)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.007<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn3" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn5" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="oead021-tblfn1"><p>Data presented as median and interquartile range.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn2"><p>BMI, body mass index; ACEI, angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors; ARB, angiotension receptor blocker; NT-proBNP, N-terminal pro b-type natriuretic peptide.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn3"><label>a</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn4"><label>b</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. non-HFpEF.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn5"><label>c</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 non-HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>Comparison of CMR parameters across the groups is listed in <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref></italic>. Left ventricular and RV volumes and EF showed no significant difference (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref></italic>). However, there was significantly lower LVMi in normal volunteers and larger LAVi, LA area index (LAAi), and RA area index (RAAi) in HFpEF patients (see <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material online</xref>, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Figures S1&#x02013;S3</xref></italic>). Left atrial area index and LAVi remained statistically significant when logistic regression modelling was performed with age included (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.001). Late gadolinium enhancement was present in 10 of the suspected HFpEF patients, which were included in final analysis, but all these were minor and occurred at the RV insertion points only.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="oead021-T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance parameters</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col valign="top" align="left" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 53)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal volunteers (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 17)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Non-HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 38)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">LV volumes and function</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV EDV index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">69.4 (64.2&#x02013;79.6)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.1 (63.0&#x02013;71.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">72.6 (61.5&#x02013;80.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.513</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV ESV index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">26.2 (22.4&#x02013;34.6)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">25.5 (23.3&#x02013;27.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.1 (19.9&#x02013;30.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.298</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV mass index (g/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">52.6 (43.5&#x02013;59.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">40.5 (39.1&#x02013;46.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">51.1 (44.6&#x02013;57.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.008<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn8" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV EF (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.2 (56.1&#x02013;66.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.5 (60&#x02013;67.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.8 (60.2&#x02013;67.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.146</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV cardiac index (L/min/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.95 (2.74&#x02013;3.45)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.77 (2.49&#x02013;3.04)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.98 (2.54&#x02013;3.53)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.168</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">RV volumes and function</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV EDV index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">76.6 (67.3&#x02013;86.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">71.6 (67.4&#x02013;79.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.8 (65.7&#x02013;80.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.143</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV ESV index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.7 (22.5&#x02013;43.3)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.2 (26.9&#x02013;31.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.6 (25.6&#x02013;36.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.281</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV EF (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">55.8 (48.6&#x02013;67.6)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">57.8 (54.4&#x02013;62.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">59.7 (53.8&#x02013;65.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.457</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Atrial size and strain</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA Volume Index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.9 (47.5&#x02013;86.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">42.6 (35.5&#x02013;53.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">43.7 (35.2&#x02013;50.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA area index (cm<sup>2</sup>/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.3 (13.6&#x02013;21.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.53 (11.5&#x02013;15.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.8 (11.5&#x02013;14.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RA area index (cm<sup>2</sup>/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2 (11.6&#x02013;16.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.8 (10.4&#x02013;12.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.0 (9.2&#x02013;13.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA reservoir strain</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.0 (11.0&#x02013;25.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">35.7 (21.4&#x02013;39.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.7 (24.0&#x02013;35.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA booster strain</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.4 (7.0&#x02013;14.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">16.9 (11.8&#x02013;19.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.7 (12.6&#x02013;21.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA conduit strain</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">8,7 (4.9&#x02013;12.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">18.1 (8.9&#x02013;20.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2 (9.1&#x02013;16.6)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn7" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn9" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="oead021-tblfn6"><p>EDV, end-diastolic volume; EF, ejection fraction; ESV, end-systolic volume; HFpEF, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; LA, left atrium; LV, left ventricle; RA, right atrium; RV, right ventricle.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn7"><label>a</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn8"><label>b</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 non-HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn9"><label>c</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. non-HFpEF.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<xref rid="oead021-T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>
</italic> compares the CMR-FT parameters for LV, RV, and tagging parameters across the four groups. There were significant differences between the CMR-FT and tagging radial and circumferential strain and SR parameters. Longitudinal strain and SR did not show any significant difference but there was a trend towards significance. Right ventricular longitudinal and radial strain showed no significant difference across the three groups.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="oead021-T3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Cardiovascular magnetic resonance feature tracking, and tagging parameters compared across the groups comprising heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, normal volunteers, and non-heart failure with preserved ejection fraction subjects</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col valign="top" align="left" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 53)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal volunteers (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 17)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Non-HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 38)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">CMR-FT</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">26.9 (21.3&#x02013;36.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">36.4 (29.3&#x02013;39.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30.6 (26.8&#x02013;33.9)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.008<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn12" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.13 (0.88&#x02013;1.59)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.76 (1.39&#x02013;2.04)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.36 (1.04&#x02013;1.65)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.004<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn12" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">16.5 (14.1&#x02013;19.7)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.2 (17.4&#x02013;21.3)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.8 (16.3&#x02013;19.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.003<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn12" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.60 (0.48&#x02013;0.73)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.81 (0.68&#x02013;1.00)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.62 (0.50&#x02013;0.75)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.008<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn12" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Longitudinal strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.9 (12.6&#x02013;16.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">16.0 (14.6&#x02013;17.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.9 (13.1&#x02013;16.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.141</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Longitudinal early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.58 (0.46&#x02013;0.68)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.70 (0.52&#x02013;0.83)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.50 (0.43&#x02013;0.65)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.068</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">23.5 (15.9&#x02013;32.3)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">22.0 (19.3&#x02013;29.0)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.2 (17.7&#x02013;27.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.696</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV longitudinal strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.5 (14.8&#x02013;26.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.4 (11.1&#x02013;27.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.9 (18.2&#x02013;24.2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.701</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Tagging</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.6 (11.4&#x02013;15.8)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.0 (15.9&#x02013;18.1)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.9 (13.9&#x02013;17.5)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.001<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn13" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></sup></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">22.5 (19.3&#x02013;25.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">19.8 (18.6&#x02013;20.4)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">23.4 (20.0&#x02013;25.6)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.011<sup><xref rid="oead021-tblfn11" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-tblfn12" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></sup></td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="oead021-tblfn10"><p>Strain and strain rate parameters are stated as positive numbers.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn11"><label>a</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn12"><label>b</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 non-HFpEF vs. normal.</p></fn><fn id="oead021-tblfn13"><label>c</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 HFpEF vs. non-HFpEF.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="oead021-s2.2"><title>Cardiac magnetic resonance parameters to diagnose heart failure with preserved ejection fraction</title><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking LV and RV strain parameters showed poor diagnostic accuracy with the highest AUC of 0.603 for circumferential strain to identify HFpEF patients from non-HFpEF patients (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref></italic>). Tagging circumferential strain and radial strain also showed poor diagnostic power (AUC 0.644 and 0.541, respectively) (<italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref></italic>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="oead021-T4"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Accuracy of cardiac magnetic resonance and echocardiography parameters to identify heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 53) from non-heart failure with preserved ejection fraction patients (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 38)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col valign="top" align="left" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/><col valign="top" align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AUC (CI)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sensitivity (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Specificity (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Diagnostic accuracy (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cut-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Feature tracking of left ventricle</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.602 (0.475&#x02013;0.713)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">66.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30.0</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.581 (0.455&#x02013;0.695)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">55.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">66.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.33</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.603 (0.477&#x02013;0.714)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">68.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.2</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.517 (0.401&#x02013;0.645)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">59.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.43</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Longitudinal strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.522 (0.412&#x02013;0.652)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.5</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Longitudinal early diastolic SR (1/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.568 (0.446&#x02013;0.692)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">47.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">73.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.49</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Feature tracking of right ventricle</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.530 (0.409&#x02013;0.651)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">8.8</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RV longitudinal strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.501 (0.387&#x02013;0.629)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">98.1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">8.4</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Feature tracking of left atrium</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA reservoir (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.804 (0.714&#x02013;0.893)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">81.1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">68.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">25.9</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA booster (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.746 (0.649&#x02013;0.847)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">76.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">69.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.6</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA conduit (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.731 (0.619&#x02013;0.831)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.8</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Atrial size</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA volume index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.776 (0.681&#x02013;0.871)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">82.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">73.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">44.6</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA area index on four-chamber (cm/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.815 (0.730&#x02013;0.901)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">66.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">86.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">74.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;RA area index on four-chamber (cm/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.700 (0.595&#x02013;0.812)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">83.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">57.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">72.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.3</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Tagging strain parameters</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Circumferential strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.644 (0.527&#x02013;0.761)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">55.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">65.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.9</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Radial strain (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.541 (0.420&#x02013;0.662)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">69.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">42.1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24.6</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">CMR LV myocardial mass</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV myocardial mass index (g/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.523 (0.415&#x02013;0.656)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">87.2</td></tr><tr><td colspan="6" rowspan="1">Echocardiography parameters</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Septal wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; (m/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.555 (0.426&#x02013;0.663)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.14</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019; (m/s)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.579 (0.470&#x02013;0.706)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">59.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.05</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Average <italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; ratio</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.584 (0.451&#x02013;0.692)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">84.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">9.3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Tricuspid regurgitation peak velocity (ms)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.768 (0.661&#x02013;0.861)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">79.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">71.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LV mass index (g/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.621 (0.510&#x02013;0.745)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">96.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">74.6</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;LA volume index (mL/m<sup>2</sup>)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.766 (0.666&#x02013;0.860)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">56.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">89.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">46.0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="oead021-tblfn14"><p>CI, confidence intervals; LA, left atrium; LV, left ventricle; RA, right atrium; RV, right ventricle; SR, strain rate.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>Left atrial reservoir strain, LAAi (on four-chamber view), and LAVi showed the highest AUCs of all the CMR parameters (AUC 0.803, 0.815, and 0.776, respectively). Adjustment was made for atrial fibrillation, giving an AUC of 0.804, 0.814, and 0.777, respectively. Left atrial booster and CoS showed satisfactory accuracy with AUCs of 0.746 and 0.731, respectively.</p><p>Right atrial area index showed satisfactory accuracy (AUC 0.700). Multivariable logistic regression analyses showed LA ResS [OR 0.90 (0.83&#x02013;0.97), <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.005], LAAi [OR 1.41 (1.13&#x02013;1.76), <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.002], and LAVi [OR 1.06 (1.02&#x02013;1.10), <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.006] were independent predictive variables of HFpEF (see <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material online</xref>, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Tables S1&#x02013;3</xref></italic>).</p><p>Left atrial reservoir strain, LAAi, and LAVi showed significantly higher diagnostic accuracy than all the CMR-FT parameters (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.01) (see <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="oead021-F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3</xref></italic>) as well as tagging radial strain (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.01). Left atrial area index was trending towards significance in comparison with tagging circumferential strain (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.094).</p><fig position="float" id="oead021-F3" fig-type="figure"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Receiver operating characteristic comparing left atrial reservoir (AUC 0.804), left atrial area index (AUC 0.815), and left atrial volume index (AUC 0.776) against the cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking left ventricle parameters. CS, circumferential strain; CDSR, circumferential early diastolic strain rate; LS, longitudinal strain; LDSR, longitudinal early diastolic strain rate; LV, left ventricle; RS, radial strain; RDSR, radial early diastolic strain rate.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="oead021f3" position="float"/></fig><p>Based on one ESC echocardiography criteria to confirm HFpEF in this study, 85 out of 91 suspected HFpEF patients fulfilled at least one echocardiography HFpEF criteria. Cardiac magnetic resonance LA ResS, LAVi, and LAAi showed correct reclassifications of 22, 18, and 27 as non-HFpEF patients, respectively, out of the 85 patients confirmed by echocardiography. The sensitivity, specificity, and diagnostic accuracy of LA ResS were 81.1%, 68.8%, 76.5%; LAVi 81.1%, 56.3%, 71.8%; and LAAi 66.0%, 84.4%, 72.9% in this group of patients (see <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material online</xref>, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Tables S4&#x02013;S6</xref></italic>). Six out of 91 suspected HFpEF patients did not fulfil any echocardiography criteria for HFpEF and were correctly diagnosed by echocardiography as non-HFpEF (see <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material online</xref>, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Tables S7&#x02013;S9</xref></italic>). Left atrial reservoir strain and LAVi incorrectly reclassified two and one patient, respectively. Left atrial area index did not reclassify any cases and thus had 100% accuracy.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s2.3"><title>Echocardiography parameters to diagnose heart failure with preserved ejection fraction</title><p>As a comparison to the CMR parameters, the best echocardiography parameters were LAVi (AUC 0.766) and TRPV (AUC 0.768). Lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019;, septal wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019;, average <italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; ratio, and LV mass index had poor diagnostic power to identify HFpEF. However, it should be noted that only one HFpEF patient was confirmed as HFpEF on the basis on LAVi alone indicating that CMR LAVi and LAAi are not replicating echocardiography results. Correlation between CMR LAVi and echocardiography LAVi was r = 0.864, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001. Forty-five HFpEF patients met the <italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; criteria for HFpEF. <italic toggle="yes">E/e</italic>&#x02019; ratio and LV mass index had significantly lower accuracy than CMR LA ResS, LAVi, and LAAi (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.01).</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s2.4"><title>Reproducibility</title><p>Intraclass correlation coefficient for LA ResS, LA volume index, LA and RA atrial area index, LV radial, circumferential and LS, and SR showed excellent agreement with intraclass correlation coefficient &#x02265;0.84 for all parameters. See <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material online</xref>, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Table S10</xref></italic>.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="oead021-s3"><title>Discussion</title><p>Our study demonstrates that LA ResS, LAAi, and LAVi are the best parameters for diagnosing HFpEF and were better at diagnosing HFpEF than CMR-FT parameters. In suspected HFpEF patients with at least one echocardiography criteria fulfilling HFpEF criteria, LA ResS, LAAi, and LAVi may have a role in correctly reclassifying patients as non-HFpEF. However, tagging and LV myocardial mass index were not helpful in diagnosing HFpEF. As far as we are aware, this is the first study to directly use CMR parameters which have been proposed as possible tools to identify HFpEF to differentiate a group of suspected HFpEF patients from non-HFpEF patients which is a frequent clinical conundrum. Our study was not designed to look for HFpEF mimics, as this has previously been assessed by Kanagala <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. 2018 and showed 27% of patients had alternative diagnoses.</p><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking has significant advantages over tagging. Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking does not require additional sequences which lengthens the CMR examination. As far as we are aware, there is no study which has looked at CMR-FT to diagnose HFpEF. Van Roeder <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. performed a small study looking at CMR-FT LA strain in HFpEF (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 22) and non-HFpEF patients (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 12). They showed a significant difference in LA ResS and CoS between the two groups but did not look into the diagnostic accuracy of the LA strain.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref></sup> Furthermore, Chirinos <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. showed that LA CoS, ResS, and LA conduit strain rate were predictive of worse outcome in a combined grouping of heart failure and non-heart failure patients after multivariable regression analysis.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref></sup> Another study by Mordi <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. showed that LS by echocardiography was a good discriminator between HFpEF and hypertension patients (AUC 0.78).<sup><xref rid="oead021-B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref></sup> This AUC is higher than the best CMR-FT parameters in our study. However, the group being compared was asymptomatic hypertensive patients whilst in our comparator group, we had non-HFpEF patients who were suspected to have HFpEF which is more representative of real-world practice where clinicians are uncertain if a patient has HFpEF. This likely reduced the accuracy of the CMR-FT parameters to identify HFpEF. Furthermore, the 2021 European Society of Cardiology guidelines<sup><xref rid="oead021-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> for diagnosing HFpEF specifically states that echocardiography LS &#x0003c;16% has a sensitivity and specificity 62% and 56% to diagnose HFpEF and is not listed as a recommended parameter for diagnosing HFpEF.</p><p>Cardiac magnetic resonance feature tracking in HFpEF.patients has been shown to correlate well with invasive diastolic functional indices. In a small study of 18 HFpEF patients and 18 age- and sex-matched healthy controls, global LS was the CMR-FT parameter which correlated most with tau.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref></sup> In this study, global LS was not assessed for its diagnostic accuracy in identifying HFpEF patients. In our study, global LS had an AUC of 0.522, which is very poor in its diagnostic accuracy. Possible explanations for this is that global LS is commonly reduced in patients with established cardiovascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes<sup><xref rid="oead021-B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="oead021-B31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref></sup> but as these are present in &#x0003e;90% of non-HFpEF patients, this likely reduced the diagnostic accuracy of global LS. In addition, global LS has been shown to be lower in older patients<sup><xref rid="oead021-B32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref></sup> and our HFpEF and non-HFpEF cohort are predominantly of an older age; thus, this may have further reduced the diagnostic power of global LS. In addition, the ESC 2021 guidelines does not recommend echocardiography global LS as it has a sensitivity of 62% and 56% compared to invasive testing.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref></sup></p><p>Tagging has long been proposed as a possible tool for assessing diastolic function.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>,<xref rid="oead021-B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref></sup> These have shown promise in identifying diastolic dysfunction;<sup><xref rid="oead021-B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref></sup> however, in terms of actually identifying HFpEF, this, as far as we are aware, has not been done before. In echocardiography, multiple parameters are usually combined in order to identify diastolic dysfunction.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup> Interestingly some of the echocardiography parameters on their own also showed poor diagnostic accuracy such as septal and lateral wall <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>&#x02019;. Therefore, it is possible that more investigation needs to be done to assess whether multiple CMR parameters combined can improve diagnostic accuracy of HFpEF. Mordi <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. looked into tagging for diagnosing HFpEF but their study found that tagging circumferential strain was not significant for identifying HFpEF and would seem to correlate with our findings.</p><p>In terms of the future, we have shown that LA ResS, LAAi, and LAVi are good parameters for diagnosing HFpEF. The need for more complimentary non-invasive diagnostic tests is evident in our study as more than &#x0003e;40% of suspected HFpEF patients were non-HFpEF despite standard investigations of NT-proBNP and transthoracic echocardiography combined with stress echocardiography/pressure measurements. Larger prospective studies are required to validate these findings of utilizing CMR parameters in patients suspected of having HFpEF. This is important as echocardiography remains the non-invasive imaging of choice for HFpEF diagnosis and the alternatives include invasive procedures such as invasive pressure measurements. Thus, an alternative non-invasive imaging modality like CMR is ideally suited to complement echocardiography especially when echocardiography images are suboptimal or there are concerns of an alternative diagnosis.</p><sec id="oead021-s3.1"><title>Limitations</title><p>Firstly, this is a small study but this prospective study covered several health districts in Hong Kong and utilized catheter pressure measurements and stress echocardiography to confirm the diagnosis of HFpEF or non-HFpEF. Furthermore, the echocardiogram and CMR examinations were performed within 24&#x02005;h of each other making the echocardiography and CMR parameters comparable and confirming that the CMR variables measured are reflective of the patient&#x02019;s HFpEF or non-HFpEF status at the time of the examination. Secondly, we were unable to age-match HFpEF patients with normal volunteers. This is due to the low number of normal volunteers who have no cardiovascular risk factors beyond the age of 70 and HFpEF patients generally being more prevalent in the elderly. However, logistic regression showed that after adjustment, LA ResS, LAAi, and LAVi are still significantly different between HFpEF and non-HFpEF patients. A third limitation of this study is that stress echocardiography/invasive catheter pressure measurements were not acquired in all patients; thus, some non-HFpEF patients may in fact be HFpEF patients. With regard to invasive pressure measurements, these were only performed at rest rather than the gold standard of under exercise conditions. Fourth, our study did not use dedicated LA cine views to obtain LA volumes, area, and strain. This has been shown to be significantly different in a recent study.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref></sup> Fifth, we did not perform echocardiography left atrial strain. Furthermore, our study was not designed to look for alternative diagnoses to HFpEF, as this has previously been assessed by Kanagala <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>.<sup><xref rid="oead021-B34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref></sup> and showed 27% of patients had alternative diagnoses. In addition, pulmonary function tests were not routinely performed in patients but where it was performed, this was utilized to exclude patients with severe pulmonary disease. Lastly, 3D echo analyses were not performed.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="oead021-s4"><title>Conclusions</title><p>This is the first prospective case-control study demonstrating that LA ResS, LAVi, and LAAi are the best CMR parameters for diagnosing HFpEF amongst clinically suspected HFpEF patients. Tagging, CMR-FT LV, and CMR-FT RV parameters may not be as useful as initially thought at diagnosing HFpEF. Larger prospective studies are required to validate these findings.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Material</title><supplementary-material id="sup1" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>oead021_Supplementary_Data</label><media xlink:href="oead021_supplementary_data.docx"><caption><p>Click here for additional data file.</p></caption></media></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><ack id="ack1"><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>We are immensely grateful to the staff and the students of the Department of Diagnostic Radiology, our cardiology colleagues from United Christian Hospital, Tseung Kwan O Hospital, and Ruttonjee Hospital who have helped with this study.</p></ack><sec id="oead021-s6"><title>Lead author biography</title><p>
<inline-graphic xlink:href="oead021il1.jpg"/>Ming-Yen Ng, BMBS, FRCR, FSCMR, FACC, FSCCT, is a clinical associate professor at the Department of Diagnostic Radiology, The University of Hong Kong; Division Chief of Cardiac Imaging, HKU&#x02013; Shenzhen Hospital, China, and HKU Cardiac Imaging Massive Open Online Course Director. He graduated from the University of Nottingham, UK, in 2005. He finished radiology training in Manchester, UK, with sub-specialization in cardiothoracic radiology in 2013. Subsequently, he completed a 1-year cardiac imaging fellowship at Toronto General Hospital before taking up his current post in Hong Kong. His research interests are quantitative stress CMR, diabetes, HFpEF, and COVID-19.</p></sec><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="oead021-s5"><title>Data availability</title><p>The data underlying this article will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author.</p></sec><sec id="oead021-s7"><title>Supplementary material</title><p>
<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary material</xref> is available at <italic toggle="yes">European Heart Journal Open</italic> online.</p></sec><sec><title>Funding</title><p>The study was supported by the Health and Medical Research Fund, Hong Kong (No. 05162736).</p><p>
<bold>Conflict of interest:</bold> M.Y.N. has received funding from Circle CVI, Arterys, TeraRecon, Lode, GE, and Bayer. All other authors have no conflict of interests to declare.</p></sec><ref-list id="ref1"><title>References</title><ref id="oead021-B1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal">
